You can add a normal HTML form to that page and wire it to your Apollo inbound router by matching the field names and adding Apollo’s JavaScript snippet to your site’s code.[1][2]

## Overall steps

1. In Apollo, open your inbound router → Share → “Integrate with your website form,” add `peopleplacers.us` as an allowed domain, and go to the field‑mapping screen (like in your screenshot). Copy the JS snippet Apollo gives you and keep that window open.[2][1]
2. On your WordPress/GoDaddy/website builder page `https://peopleplacers.us/project-solutions/`, add a form (via your page builder or raw HTML) that collects at least the required fields from Apollo: work email, first name, last name, company name, company location, and audit history, plus any others you want.[3][1]

## Match Apollo fields to your form

Apollo matches by the HTML `name` attribute, not the label text.[1][2]

- For each inbound form field (Work Email, First name, etc.), set the corresponding input’s `name` on your website form.  
  - Example HTML (conceptually):  
    - Work Email → `<input type="email" name="email" />`  
    - First name → `<input type="text" name="first_name" />`  
    - Last name → `<input type="text" name="last_name" />`  
    - Company name → `<input type="text" name="company_name" />`  
    - Company location (single‑select) → `<select name="company_location">...</select>`  
    - Audit History (single‑select) → `<select name="audit_history">...</select>`  
- In the Apollo mapping screen, paste each of those `name` values into the “Your website form fields” column so every required field shows as mapped.[2]

If Apollo defines a field as a single‑select with specific options (like your “Company size” and “Audit History”), be sure your website select options use the same choices Apollo expects.[1][2]

## Add the Apollo script to your site

1. Take the JS snippet from the “Integrate with your website form” area and paste it into your website’s code, usually in the global header or between `<head>` tags so it loads on every page (or at least on `/project-solutions/`).[2][1]
2. Make sure the `appId` and `schedulingLink` values in that snippet remain exactly as provided by Apollo.[1]

## Connect form submission to Apollo

With the script on the page, you tell Apollo to submit your form data when the user clicks your form’s submit button.[1]

- Simplest version (if this is the first and only form on the page):  
  - Add a small inline script or custom JS that calls `window.ApolloMeetings.submit()` on submit; when called with no parameters it will grab the first form element and send mapped fields into Apollo, triggering the router and calendar flow.[1]
- If you have multiple forms, you can either:  
  - Make your intake form the first form on the page, or  
  - Pass a reference to that specific form into `ApolloMeetings.submit({ form: yourFormElement })` as shown in Apollo’s examples.[1]

