Use WordPress on your GoDaddy/cPanel hosting to place both the Apollo script (in the header) and the intake form (in the page content).[1][2][3]

## Where to put the Apollo script

- Because you are on GoDaddy WordPress, the cleanest path is to use a “Header & Footer”–type plugin (or your theme’s custom code panel) and paste the Apollo JS snippet into the global **head** area so it loads on every page.[2][4]
- If you prefer to edit files directly in cPanel, copy `header.php` into a child theme and paste the Apollo snippet before the closing `</head>` tag; the child theme’s `header.php` will override the original.[5][1]

## How to add the form on `/project-solutions/`

- In WordPress, edit the “Project Solutions” page, add a **Custom HTML** block, and paste your form HTML with the fields Apollo expects (email, first_name, last_name, company_name, company_location, audit_history, etc.).[6][3]
- Make sure the `name` attributes in your inputs/selects exactly match what you mapped in Apollo’s inbound router screen so Apollo can read them when `window.ApolloMeetings.submit()` runs.[3]

## Triggering Apollo from WordPress form submit

- In that same page (or globally), add a small inline script that listens for the form’s submit and calls `window.ApolloMeetings.submit()`; this will grab the first form on the page and push the data into Apollo, firing your inbound router.[3]
- If you later have multiple forms, pass the specific form element into `ApolloMeetings.submit({ form: document.getElementById('your-form-id') })` so only that intake form is routed.[3]

